Z||000004||86SE||08-28-2000||12:24 AM||||What ever you do, do not buy a WinModem, thats what I have and I have to restart my computer everyother time I connect!<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||08-28-2000||03:46 AM||||That's right, whatever you do don't get a WinModem. This will slow down your computer considerably whenever you are online. I always opt for external modems. You can get USB modems now for a very low price.||||reg||1
Z||000006||whamel||08-28-2000||08:31 AM||||     Not to mention the fact that if you ever want to play a DOS based game over the modem...you're out of luck!  I hate winmodems - they are incompatible with everything I seem to want to do!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>That's right, whatever you do don't get a WinModem. This will slow down your computer considerably whenever you are online. I always opt for external modems. You can get USB modems now for a very low price.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-29-2000||12:00 AM||||Winmodems are inherently inferior but, did you try updating the drivers? That can also cause your problem. I deal with allot of these things. It's almost all PC makers ship with now. Even big brands like Compaq and IBM. Driver problems can really hose them.<P>Update other drivers as well ONE AT A TIME! I've seen video and sound driver interfere with other stuff.<P>Also try a diferant cord between the modem and wall. Even tho it works in the other computer. I've seen some cords and jacks not fit quite right. (Happens with network cards to.)<P>Also.... Make sure you get a modem line "surge protector." I've seen a number of computers fried thru the modem. Allot of the new modems aren't as well protected from the phone line as they used to be. You're actually in more danger from the phone line than the power with these modems. These modems just barely meet the legal minimum. Even the big names are buying them because they are so cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||08-29-2000||12:15 AM||||i use to own a winmodem until i returned it.<BR>Any modem running off software, or your CPU isn't worth it.<P>Get a US robotics or some other modem for 30 bucks thats 56k X2 and some other abbreviations. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||LarryB||08-29-2000||11:51 PM||||You want a modem that conforms to the V.90 specs. X2 and K56flex were proprietary and now obsolete 56Kbaud implementations that were incompatible at speeds greater than 33.6Kbaud, the best points of each were taken to develop the V.90 standard.<P>Ogre is right on in recommending that you upgrade the modem drivers to the latest version, and there's good reason for it.<P>When the V.90 spec was first released some things weren't really nailed down very well, so it was easy to release drivers or firmware that worked and was interoperable but wasn't strictly compatible with the final released version of the V.90 standard.<P>Early this year Lucent rolled out a firmware upgrade for their modem banks that strictly enforced the final V.90 spec.  These modem banks (Lucent TNT) are commonly used with larger ISPs as well as the bulk dialup providers reselling service to providers ranging from AOL/Juno/NetZero through regionals like RCN down to local service providers.<P>Those people who had Winmodems with early versions of the drivers found themselves SOL.  Their modems didn't strictly conform with the spec so they couldn't stay connected, which meant they couldn't search for and download new drivers.  If I remember right the dial pool would often drop the connection after about 800 bytes of traffic had passed. <P>Lucent got a knee shoved into their back and implemented a workaround that works in a majority but not all cases, and the correct firmware may or may not be installed in a particular modem bank. The correct fix is to download and install an updated driver for your winmodem that strictly and correctly conforms with the V.90 spec. <P>For lightish usage like email or web browsing a Winmodem is OK.  If you're a hardcore gamer they're definitely the wrong choice, a 3Com/US Robotics or equivalent quality modem is a better fit.  I prefer to use external modems with cable to a serial port but thats largely personal preference. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||08-30-2000||12:03 AM||||Also check modem settings. You can force the modem to run slower. set it down below 33.6 and see if it connects.<P>There are AT commands or other settings that will disable the digital mode. That would temporarily solve the problem by forcing the modem to run at the older 33.6 standards instead of the 56k ones. Depends on the modem exactly what you can set. find the book for it if you have one. Could be as simple as locking the maximum connect rate but I wouldn't count on it.||||reg||1
